cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If this repository helps you, you may cite it as below."
title: "reader-ergonomics"
version: "1.19.1"
date-released: "2026-10-29"
abstract: >-
  A synthesis of what cognitive science, psycholinguistics and software
  engineering report about writing notes, documents and files that are cheap
  to read, for people and for language models. Each principle carries its
  grade of evidence, many are untested conventions, and every topic lists the
  evidence against. The literature reviews were drafted with AI agents; every
  reference was resolved by script and claims were spot-checked, and a number
  read only in a summary is marked as secondary. It is an informal synthesis
  of published research, written with Claude, an AI assistant by Anthropic: not
  an official standard, not peer-reviewed, and not a new result. Use it as a
  reading list and a starting point, not as an authority.
type: software
authors:
  - name: "Tewf"
